A cold start at a tight tolerance fails its first step on MinStepSize.
Found 2026-11-06, measuring SuppressAlgebraicError's accuracy cost.

  With the algebraic rows in IDA's error test, 9 of the 17 regression configs
  fail with IDA_ERR_FAIL at tolerances 1000x their own, and Nonlin2 and
  nonlin_ss already at 10x: each first step needs h below the default
  MinStepSize of 1e-7 (about 7e-8 for Nonlin2). The SUNDIALS log shows dsm
  falling in proportion to h, which is not the inconsistent-state signature: on
  a cold start IDA_YA_YDP_INIT leaves the algebraic y' at zero, so the
  predictor's error in sigma, q, lambda and phi is h |y'|, first order where
  the test expects second. Suppressing the algebraic error, as a run with a
  field now does by default, runs every one of them; so does MinStepSize = 1e-12.

  To decide: lower MinStepSize's default, or give the algebraic unknowns a
  starting y' on a cold start as a projected restart already does
  (consistentAlgebraicDerivatives, after IDACalcIC), which would also let IDA
  take the first step its tolerance allows rather than one the zeros force.


Move the built documentation out of this repository.
Raised 2026-09-30, when the build stopped writing into the source tree.

  Every build now writes only into its build directory, docs included: the
  `docs` target renders into <build>/docs/html. The one exception is
  `cmake --install`, whose docs component also copies that HTML into the
  checkout at docs/_build/html (MANTA_DOCS_SOURCE_COPY), because that is where
  a local copy has always been looked for. It is gitignored, so it is a
  convenience rather than anything the repository carries.

  The end state is a separate git repository that holds *only* built HTML and
  exists only to back manta-docs.readthedocs.io: the `docs` component installs
  into a checkout of it, a commit there is a published version, and this
  repository holds the sources alone. Read the Docs then serves static HTML
  instead of running Sphinx itself, which also takes .readthedocs.yaml's pins
  (the sphinx<8 hold for sphinx-material) and its intersphinx-timeout failure
  mode out of the publishing path -- the build that publishes is the build that
  was checked locally with -W.

  To decide: whether RTD is still the right host once it is only serving
  files (GitHub Pages from that repository would do the same), who pushes to
  it (a CI job on main, most likely -- the Docs leg already builds the HTML, so
  publishing is one more step there), and how versions map to branches or tags
  there. Once it exists, MANTA_DOCS_SOURCE_COPY defaults to that checkout,
  or goes, and docs/_build/ leaves .gitignore.


Revisit a better error measure for the discretisation.
Raised 2026-11-06; the last attempt stalled on a choice rather than a defect.

  The run has no calibrated measure of its own discretisation error. The
  modal-decay sensor ranks cells and degrees but is not an error;
  ||u* - u_h|| under-predicts the true error by up to 250x on Shestakov, worse
  with resolution, and a ladder's two-level difference over-predicts by up to
  12x (PERFORMANCE.md, "Why the tolerance is not chosen automatically"; commit
  9be3e48). The dual-weighted estimate is calibrated, but only for a named
  objective.

  What blocked the last attempt was a choice of error, not an implementation:
  for steady-state diffusion the error in q drives the error in u, so which
  quantity the measure is taken on -- u, q, the flux, or a norm coupling them --
  had to be settled first and was not. Settle that before writing code; the
  answer serves whatever the measure then drives (a tolerance, mesh grading,
  the degree).


TimeMarch stops on dY/dt; the other two steady modes stop on the residual.
Found 2026-09-23, re-measuring the paper's steady-cost table at a tolerance a
plasma transport run would actually use.

  PseudoTransient and Newton bound ||F|| by SteadyStateTolerance. TimeMarch
  bounds the trace's change over one output interval, in weights
  1 / (SteadyStateTolerance |lambda| + AbsoluteTolerance) -- so for a trace of
  order one the floor, AbsoluteTolerance, governs, and SteadyStateTolerance
  barely moves where it stops: at the default floor of 1e-3, Park, Jardin and
  Shestakov report the same 120, 183 and 257 visits from 1e-11 to 1e-2. The
  floor was a hard-coded 1e-3 until 2026-11-06; it is AbsoluteTolerance now,
  which is what tightens a TimeMarch stop.

  So the three methods still cannot be put on a common accuracy target by one
  key, which is what a cost comparison between them wants. At 1e-2 the
  residual-driven methods stop at 1.7e-3 on Jardin while TimeMarch runs on to
  3.8e-16, so the ratio between them flatters the steady solvers; the paper's
  table carries the errors beside the visits and quotes the matched-accuracy
  factors separately.

  To decide: whether TimeMarch's test should be on the residual instead, which
  would make SteadyStateTolerance mean one thing across all three modes at the
  cost of changing what every existing TimeMarch run stops at.


g++-14 miscompiles this tree at -O3 -flto -march=native. Root cause not found.
Found 2026-08-23. CLAUDE.md has the summary; this is the reproduction.

  To reproduce, on g++-14 (14.2.0 here) on an AVX2 host:

    1. Check out main. Add one inert member to SystemSolver, in the private
       block next to `steadyDiagnostics`:

           bool reproPaddingFlag = false;
           std::vector<double> reproPadding;

       Nothing reads either. Add `#include <vector>` with the other includes.
    2. `rm -rf build && cmake --preset default && cmake --build build`
    3. Run the one test in a loop:

           for i in $(seq 1 12); do
             Tests/UnitTests/UnitTests --run_test=algebraic_derivative_tests/\
               the_assembled_jacobian_matches_a_finite_difference_of_the_residual
           done

    Without the member: 12/12 pass. With it: 8/12 fail, always the same way --
    the AuxDiffusion cases, plain and superconvergent, at k = 1, 2 and 3, drift
    ~1.24.

    NOTE: `algebraic_derivative_tests` no longer exists -- it went with the dG/dt
    gate, the only thing that read the algebraic time derivatives -- so step 3
    cannot be run as written. The miscompilation was never diagnosed, only
    bounded, so this needs a fresh reproduction rather than deletion. What
    mattered was a dense finite-difference of residual() inlined into a test
    translation unit plus a change to SystemSolver's member layout;
    SolveJacTests.cpp differences the residual the same way and is where to
    start.

  The signature, which is how to recognise it if it turns up elsewhere: the
  differenced Jacobian loses the column of the first interior trace DOF
  (index 33 / 49 / 65 at k = 1 / 2 / 3, always lambda[1]), giving 0 against a
  stable assembled -1.5, so the drift is 1.5 / 1.2071067811865475 =
  1.2426406871192851. Passing runs are bit-identical to each other; failing runs
  all differ from each other.

  Established:

  * Needs all three of g++-14, `-flto` and `-march=native` (AVX2, znver2 here).
    Drop any one -> 10/10 clean. `-O0` clean. g++-15 clean. clang++-19 clean.
    (g++-13 does not build this tree, so it was not tested.)
  * Not build staleness, not leftover output files, not ASLR (`setarch -R` with a
    fixed environment still flakes), not BLAS threading, not uninitialised
    trivial automatics (`-ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero` and `=pattern` both still
    flake). AddressSanitizer: 12/12 clean, no invalid access.
  * The solver binary is *not* observably affected: `MaNTA` on
    Tests/RegressionTests/AuxVarTest.conf gave bit-identical netCDF over 8 runs
    with the member in place. `MaNTA` is built without -DTEST from a different
    object set, which points at the header-only `fdjac::jacobian` inlined into
    the test TU rather than at the solver.

  The obstacle, and the thing to know before spending an afternoon on it: **every
  codegen perturbation hides it.** Adding a read of Y, extending an existing
  BOOST_TEST_MESSAGE, storing into a file-scope array, `-fno-strict-aliasing`,
  `-fno-tree-vectorize` -- all take it to 10/10 clean. So a flag that "fixes" it
  is not evidence about the mechanism; `-fno-strict-aliasing` in particular looks
  like a smoking gun and is not one. The only asymmetric signal is that adding a
  member to SystemSolver reliably *creates* it.

  Next steps, roughly in order of expected value:

  * Diff the g++-14 assembly for the test TU with and without the inert member,
    around the inlined `fdjac::jacobian` loop. This is the only route left that
    does not perturb the thing being measured.
  * If that shows a genuine miscompile, reduce it and report it upstream; g++-14
    is still a supported distro compiler and CI has two required legs on it.
  * If it shows the compiler exploiting UB, the UB is ours and needs fixing
    regardless of compiler. The placement-new over `mu_` in `DGSolnImpl::Map`
    (`new (&mu_) VectorWrapper(...)`, then continued use of the name `mu_`) is
    the one piece of the mapping code that is not obviously well-defined, and is
    worth reading first even though the failing DOF is a trace rather than a
    scalar.

  Until then the build warns when it sees g++-14 (cmake/MantaCompilerFlags.cmake),
  and CI's two
  g++-14 legs should be treated as capable of failing for reasons unrelated to
  the change under review.

Left over from the TransportSystem interface work. Everything else in that plan
landed; these are the pieces that were deliberately not done, with the reason.

  - The autodiff Source still receives the *stored* sigma, not sigma_hat.
    State::sigmaHat exists and is correct, but AutodiffTransportSystem::Sources
    builds its RealVector from the stored (negated) value, so a case that starts
    using its sigma argument gets the opposite sign from the one SigmaFn
    returned. Flipping it also needs a compensating negation in
    dSources_dsigma, because autodiff would then differentiate with respect to
    sigma_hat while the solver wants d/d(stored sigma).

    The reason this is not already done: *nothing in the tree reads the incoming
    sigma at all*. AdjointPlasma threads it into Sn/Spi/Spe without using it --
    as the C++ MirrorPlasma did before it was removed -- and every
    dSources_dsigma is identically
    zero. So the change is unobservable today and no test would catch getting it
    wrong -- which is exactly why it should not be made blind. Write a physics
    case with a genuinely sigma-dependent source first, check it against a
    closed form, then flip.

  - The out-of-tree paths are verified by hand, not by CI. Both were exercised
    end to end -- a one-file C++ case compiled against the installed headers,
    dlopened via PhysicsPlugins and run; and `pip install .` into a fresh venv
    with a Python case run from the user's own directory through the `manta`
    command -- but neither is an automated test. The C++ one needs
    `cmake --install build --prefix` to a temp directory and a compiler
    invocation; the Python one needs a venv. Both are slow enough to want their
    own CI job rather than a place in `ctest`.


Found since, and left undone deliberately rather than overlooked. Each says what
would have to exist before the fix could be checked, which in every case is the
reason it was not made:

  - AutodiffTransportSystem::MMS_Source (PhysicsCases/AutodiffTransportSystem.cpp)
    still evaluates the plain, geometry-blind Flux/Source overloads directly,
    while every other hook -- SigmaFn, Sources, AuxG, and every
    dSigmaFn_.../dSources_.../dAuxG_... derivative -- goes exclusively through
    the geometry-aware overloads added for the field-model coupling. The two
    sets agree today only because the plain overload is still what every case's
    Flux/Source body actually is: nothing yet gives the geometry-aware overload
    content that differs from it. That stops being true the moment a case's real
    physics genuinely depends on geometry, because the plain overload is the
    mandatory pure virtual -- such a case is *forced* to write some body for it
    just to compile, and MMS_Source will manufacture a source against that
    fictional, geometry-blind stand-in rather than against the equation the
    solver integrates. A manufactured solution that still converges, at the
    right rate, to the wrong function, with nothing pointing at why.

    dSigmaFn_dp and dSources_dp carried the same defect and are fixed: they
    feed F_p in computeAdjointGradients, so there it was a wrong gradient beside
    a perfectly good G, which is the adjoint's own failure mode and belonged
    with the transposes. MMS_Source is not on that path and is not the same
    two-line change: it is handed (i, x, t) and nothing else, so there is no psi
    to evaluate geometry at, and AutodiffTransportSystem holds no field model
    and no route to the solver's. Threading one in is a change to the
    manufactured-solution facility, which the coupled order study owns. Write
    the manufactured coupled case that would catch the old behaviour being wrong
    before changing it.

  - FieldModel::FieldResidual receives no `states_dot`, so a field row cannot
    depend on the transport time derivatives -- and FieldResidualPrime's `dRdot`
    out-parameter, which is meant to be exactly that derivative, is therefore
    unfillable by any model that can exist. Every consistent model returns it
    zero, so the `alpha * dRdot` term the A2 assembly carries
    (SystemSolver::assembleFieldCoupling) is provably inert rather than wrong,
    and no test reaches it or can.

    Not merely a dead slot, though. A model author who finds it unfillable and
    reaches for the nearest thing that fits -- putting d(row)/d(psi') in `dRdot`
    rather than in `dRddpsidt` -- writes a psi derivative into the coupling row
    against the transport DOFs, at the wrong indices entirely. A2 is only ever
    applied, never assembled or printed, so the symptom would be slow Newton
    convergence on a coupled run and nothing else. FieldModel.hpp now says so at
    the declaration, which is where the mistake would be made.

    The fix is to give FieldResidual a `states_dot` argument, matching ScalarG,
    which takes both y and ydot for the same reason. That is an interface change
    to the FieldModel value hook, which nothing in the coupling as built owns:
    the assembly, the iterative solve and the adjoint transposes all consume the
    signature rather than setting it. Doing it wants a field model
    whose residual genuinely carries d/dt of a transport quantity, checked
    against a closed form first: the same "write the case that would catch it
    being wrong before changing it" rule the autodiff-sigma entry above records.

    **The transport rows have since gained exactly this, which makes the
    remaining work a third application of a pattern rather than a new one.** A
    source may read State::udot, reports dSources_dudot, and that block enters
    the Jacobian weighted by alpha beside the mass term -- see
    docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-21-time-derivative-sources-design.md, which
    lists the field rows as its phase 3. Two things it settled that this entry
    was going to have to settle anyway. The value travels *in* the state object
    rather than as a second argument, which is what let forty-odd existing cases
    compile unchanged; and the alpha-weighted term is provably invisible at
    alpha = 0, so a finite-difference check of it has to be written at alpha != 0
    or it gates nothing -- measured, deleting the term moves the alpha = 3.7
    check from 8.8e-10 to 0.105 and does not move the alpha = 0 one at all.
    Whatever is done for FieldResidual should follow the same two.


  - The coupled adjoint has one structurally missing term, zero by construction
    rather than by assumption:

    d(field residual)/dp. FieldModel has no notion of an adjoint parameter, so
    dR/dp is zero because there is no hook a model author could fill -- not
    because the term is small. A coil current or a boundary flux that appeared in
    both the field residual and the parameter vector would need one, and
    computeAdjointGradients would then contract it with adjoint_field, which the
    coupled solve already computes and currently uses only inside the elimination.

  - The coupled sweep uses Irons-Tuck, a rank-one secant acceleration. It is
    exact in one step for nField == 1 and any spectrum, and removes the dominant
    eigendirection for nField > 1 -- but only that one, so a coupling with two
    comparable eigenvalues outside the unit circle can still fall back to the
    exact Schur solve. The depth-m generalisation is Anderson acceleration, which
    for an affine map is GMRES on the Schur complement. **That now exists as
    FieldSolve = krylov** (couplingKrylov: SPGMR on the field increment, the
    system the sweep iterates on, in the field's error weights), at both tiers; on
    ManufacturedFieldVector at strength 40 (rho = 1.87) it reaches the exact
    solve in 5 iterations. What is left is the default: iterative stays the
    default at tier 2, and nothing yet measures krylov against it over whole
    runs. Its adjoint is GMRES too, on the transposed system
    (adjointCouplingKrylov), at both tiers.

    rho(M) was measured at 1.611 (cj = 0) and 1.571 (cj = 1e8) on
    RichGeometricDiffusion: a property of the coupling, not of the time step.
    That fixture could not be integrated on FieldSolve = iterative at all before
    the acceleration -- IDA cut h to the floor over five attempts at the first
    step, with Newton update norms of order 1e10 -- and now completes with no
    fallbacks over ~1900 Jacobian solves and reaches the finite-difference
    gradient to 1e-10.

    **Measured, the rank-one step copes better than the argument above predicts,
    and the successor is a scaling concern rather than a known gap.**
    ManufacturedFieldVector's five unknowns, scanned over coupling strengths from
    1 to 1e5 -- rho rising to just under 2 and never falling back within a cap
    of 400 -- converge every time. The steps are rank one individually but not the *same*
    rank-one direction, so the iteration is not confined to the two-dimensional
    subspace the pessimistic argument assumes. No fixture has yet been found
    where the acceleration fails outright.

    What the spread does mean is that **FieldSolveMaxSweeps = 20 is not enough
    for five field unknowns on every right-hand side**: over six right-hand
    side *directions* at strength 10 (rho = 1.57), with the sweep stopping at
    1e-4 in the fixture's error weights or a millionth of the increment, the
    counts are 10, 23, 10, 17, 13, 10, so one of the six escalates at the
    shipped default and all six converge at 60. a_divergent_five_dof_coupling_is_still_solved_exactly measures exactly
    that and asserts it, as a tripwire: it requires both some to converge and
    some to escalate, so raising the default, changing the stopping test or
    improving the accelerator has to update the test and this entry together.
    The counts depend on the run's tolerances -- the stop is in its error
    weights -- and on the right-hand side's direction, so a
    single-right-hand-side measurement of this is not a measurement.

  - Nothing latches the fallback. A genuinely divergent coupling pays
    FieldSolveMaxSweeps sweeps *and* the exact solve on every Jacobian solve for
    the whole run. Two candidate fixes, neither taken here:

      * Latch after N consecutive fallbacks. N is a number nobody can presently
        justify, and rho does drift with cj even if only by 2% in the one case
        measured.
      * Latch on *cost* rather than on failure: switch to exact once the running
        mean sweep count exceeds nField + 1, which is the break-even above. This
        is the better-founded of the two -- the threshold is derived rather than
        picked -- and it would also catch the case the fallback latch misses, a
        sweep that converges every time and still costs more than exact. It is a
        behavioural change to the default mode, so it wants deciding rather than
        slipping in beside an accelerator.

    The end-of-run count is the signal for now -- and it is printed only on the
    time-marching path, beside IDA's own totals, so a steady-state run
    (SteadyStateSolver, which does not go through IDASolve) reports nothing.

  - The objective estimate has one in-loop consumer, and not the one this entry
    was about.

    SteadyStateTest = "Objective" stops the undamped solve when each objective's
    algebraic error, read off the Newton correction, is within its share of
    ObjectiveTolerance, and AdaptationTarget = "Objective" drives the degree and
    the mesh from the end-of-solve discretisation estimate (docs/running.rst,
    docs/adaptivity.rst). What no criterion yet does is give up: "stop here, this
    cannot beat the incumbent". estimateObjective() is callable at any point and
    leaves alpha and the factorised blocks as it found them, so a continuation
    step can ask it, but solveSteadyState has no exit for that answer.
    SteadyOutcome records which exit was taken and the estimate is filled before
    a failure throws, so a caught failure carries a usable partial answer, and
    MaxContinuationSteps plus continueSteadyState() turn that into a workable
    slice-and-inspect loop: a solve cut short resumes at the state and the
    pseudo-time step it reached, at no measured cost in continuation steps. That is enough for a caller willing
    to look between slices. A clean early return from *inside* a step still needs
    adding, along with whatever criterion would drive it.

    Before adding one, note what the measurements say about the criterion. An
    early exit keyed on the *bound* is worth much less than it sounds: the
    objective's error is proportional to ||J^-1 F||, and driving ||F|| down is the
    whole of the solve, so there is no tail to cut. Deciding G to 1% still costs
    95% of the solve and even a 50% decision costs 80%. The win is in solving
    looser and correcting -- 34% cheaper and three times more accurate than
    converging two orders harder and taking G raw -- which needs no early exit at
    all, only a caller willing to set SteadyStateTolerance and read `corrected`.

    So the in-loop hook is for a criterion nobody has yet: abandoning a parameter
    point against an incumbent the caller supplies. That is worth building when
    there is a sweep to measure it on.

    The slice loop is reachable from Python as start_steady / continue_steady /
    finish_steady, wrapped as manta.SteadySolve, and through the JAX FFI on the
    same names -- so manta.SteadySolve(ffi_runner) works. CPU only, like Run and
    Run_ss; the outcome is returned as a concrete int32, which forces the sync a
    Python `while` needs and is why a slice loop belongs in eager code or inside
    an io_callback rather than under jit.

  - Two things about the estimate are unmeasured and would change its sizing.

      * The norm. The bound pairs unweighted 2-norms. Pairing the WRMS norm with
        its dual should be tighter, and is the norm IDA's own error test would
        recognise.
      * Whether it transfers to a time-marching run. Only steady solves were
        measured, and there G is a property of the fixed point rather than of a
        path, which is what makes J^-1 F the right correction.

    The stellarator's cost profile is the other open question -- a solve that
    spends many steps at low residual would change the arithmetic above -- and
    needs a machine that can hold the real resolution: the full case wants ~88 GB
    for the drift-kinetic solve, and the reduced one does not converge.

Superconvergence follow-ons (see Tests/README.md for what is already verified):

  - Paper II's HDG(ABC) / HDG+ family: W_h = P_{k+1} with tau ~ 1/h makes u_h
    itself superconverge, with no postprocessing, and reaches k+2 at k = 0 too.
    Needs mixed polynomial degrees across fields, which the single-k assumption in
    DGSoln::Map, every block in Matrices.cpp, AdjointVectors.cpp, the pybind11
    casters and the restart format all rest on. tau ~ 1/h also changes every
    existing run.


Open observations from the theta-pinch field work, recorded rather than
investigated. Each is a measurement that did not fit, with what was not tried.

  - ThetaPinch.conf converges at only about order 1.6 at k = 4 under mesh
    refinement, with the field model alone. The manufactured studies at every
    coupling tier do not show it, so it is a property of the registered model
    or of its configuration rather than of the coupling: a study of the theta
    pinch against a converged reference, by k, would say which.
  - A field grid much finer than the transport mesh stalls IDA, under
    FieldSolve = exact as well as iterative. CLAUDE.md records why the knots
    follow the mesh (p_h jumps at every face, and a finer spline resolves the
    jumps into spikes in dV'/dx); what is not known is whether the stall is that
    and nothing more.
  - The discretisation estimate refuses any field or equilibrium model, at
    every tier (DiscretisationEstimate.cpp): the enriched residual has no field
    rows, so the estimate would leave out the geometry's share. Needed before
    AdaptationTarget = "Objective" can run on a coupled case.
